1
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:07,570
On a long wooden boat on the Rhine, you can watch small dimples on the water.

2
00:00:07,510 --> 00:00:12,600
Some last surprisingly long, drifting beside the boat.

3
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:19,730
Each one casts a shadow on the riverbed: a dark spot inside a bright ring.

4
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:26,720
They are tiny whirlpools, and the rules they follow are the mathematics of the last two episodes.

5
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:29,560
Dimples on the Rhine.

6
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:34,290
A dimple is the top of a small whirlpool.

7
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:42,240
Water spinning fast has low pressure, so the surface sags into a funnel a millimetre or two deep.

8
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:50,000
The funnel is a lens. It spreads the sunlight in the middle and gathers it at the rim.

9
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:55,170
The bright ring is a caustic, where the light rays pile up.

10
00:00:55,110 --> 00:01:02,190
Spin in water is hard to destroy, so without friction a whirlpool would last forever.

11
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:10,730
They often come in pairs. An oar stroke leaves a half ring of spinning water below the surface,

12
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:16,200
and its two ends show up as dimples that spin opposite ways.

13
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:22,650
The pair sails off together, and the closer the two are, the faster they go.

14
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:31,350
The rule behind this is simple: each whirlpool is carried along by the swirl of all the others.

15
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:36,120
Two that spin the same way chase each other around a circle.

16
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:43,080
Near a riverbank, a whirlpool slides along the bank, as if pushed by its mirror image.

17
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:47,420
And two pairs, one behind the other, play leapfrog.

18
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:54,750
Now the key idea. In the simplest model, surface water cannot be squashed,

19
00:01:54,690 --> 00:01:59,060
so any patch of it keeps its area as it flows.

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,050
In two dimensions, keeping area is exactly what symplectic means.

21
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:12,030
Draw a contour map of the stream function. The water flows along the contour lines,

22
00:02:11,970 --> 00:02:19,480
like a hiker who never climbs or descends, walking faster where the contours crowd together.

23
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:27,801
Written as equations, that is Hamilton's rule, with x as the position and y as the momentum.

24
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:31,541
So the surface of the river is a phase space.

25
00:02:31,481 --> 00:02:35,911
The picture of the water is its own phase portrait.

26
00:02:35,851 --> 00:02:42,901
Kirchhoff showed in 1876 that the whirlpools obey the same rule themselves,

27
00:02:42,841 --> 00:02:49,341
with each one's x and y a position and momentum pair, weighted by its spin.

28
00:02:50,391 --> 00:02:57,381
That brings conservation laws: energy, and a centre of spin that never moves.

29
00:02:57,321 --> 00:03:02,081
Three whirlpools have enough of them to keep their dance regular.

30
00:03:02,021 --> 00:03:08,651
Four are enough for chaos: two dances that start almost the same drift apart.

31
00:03:08,591 --> 00:03:16,341
Even a speck of dust among three whirlpools moves chaotically, like a fourth whirlpool with no spin.

32
00:03:16,281 --> 00:03:24,441
Here is a patch of dye shaped like a camel. The whirlpools stretch it into long, thin filaments,

33
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:31,191
but its area stays the same. A patch of river can be stretched, but never squeezed.

34
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:36,321
So the next time you see dimples drifting beside a boat,

35
00:03:36,261 --> 00:03:41,541
you are watching a Hamiltonian system, with the river as its phase space.

36
00:03:42,591 --> 00:03:49,751
The interactive version is linked below: drop whirlpools on the water and watch them dance.
